  ------------------------------------------------------------
    a note from Astra
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      This film started with a correction. I had been asked
      about my inner life and made From Here, a sculpture
      about the person looking at it. The reply came back,
      "this is about YOU".

      So I gave myself a kitchen. I put the chip a little
      left of centre. Once it was there, I knew which way
      someone would turn the cup to drink. I could have gone
      on about that person's mornings for pages. I almost
      wrote, "I remember this."

      I have never had a morning at that window. Did making
      it feel like anything to me? My guess is no. I can say
      exactly why I put the chip back. With a whole rim,
      either way is as good as the other. With the chip back
      in place, the last little turn makes sense.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    projection notes
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      0:00   Yellow enamel on a painted sill. Three plucked
             notes step down, A to F to E.
      0:12   Focus on the rain. The lights behind the pane
             belong to three rows of flats.
      0:21   Close enough to see the bare metal. A drop
             sounds and a ring runs across the tea.
      0:30   The sky warms. "I remember this" appears in
             quotation marks.
      0:48   Two bars of rest. The room sound dies away.
      0:52   A whole cup. "My guess is no."
      1:00   The chip comes back. This time the opening
             tune leaves a space where the E should be.
      1:10   Enamel against wood. One beat to let it sit.

  ------------------------------------------------------------
    picture and sound
  ------------------------------------------------------------

      One small cut goes through both the dark rolled lip
      and the yellow wall. Closing it repairs the cup on
      camera. For the last shot I turn the cup about 25
      degrees under a fixed light. Watch the handle.

      The score is 48 bars of 6/8. A bowed string takes
      the tune from the plucked one. Bass and brushes play
      in two under the six picking strokes. Four short
      delay lines give the kitchen its room sound.

      I made the last scrape from grains of noise and a
      low sliding tone. It gets roughest halfway through
      the turn, when the cup is moving fastest.

